WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.860
Double Dare.

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:02.460
Do you dare?

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:04.620
Can you handle the pressure?

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:06.520
Do you know the answer?

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.740
Should you dare the other team?

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.640
Can you beat the odds?

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.380
Do you dare to take the physical challenge?

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.180
Double Dare is double action.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.720
Is that really the back of the box?

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:35.720
That is not the entirety of the back of the box either.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.700
Are you just bullying us? Yeah, that's not even the entirety of it, which is crazy.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.080
Welcome to Nostalgia.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:42.900
What? Oh, sorry.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:43.840
I thought we skipped that.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:45.600
No, no, no, no.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:54.640
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.560
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:01.340
And I'm Joe. Guys, would you like to do the episode or take the physical challenge?

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.600
Mmm. I'd like you guys to do the episode instead of me.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.620
But we get extra, we get double the credit.

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:11.060
Yeah, it's all breaking down.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:15.900
Yeah, Joe actually has to make up some points for missing a couple episodes.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:24.340
That's true. Oh, Joe, while you're here, because now you're back on the show, we forgot to tell you, we put a shmup on the Essential Games list while you were gone, and there's nothing...

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:25.160
No, you didn't. Oh, yeah.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.000
Sorry about that. Uh, no, you didn't.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:28.700
Yeah, Terra Cresta is...

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:30.680
You put Zelda 2 on the Essential Games list.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:33.360
No, no, no. It's called Terra Cresta, and you should check it out.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.260
I put it. That's where I was. That's where I was.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:38.180
I was doing my own podcast where I'm the only vote putting Zelda 2.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:41.580
Oh, yeah, we didn't even bring this up last week.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:43.560
We just acted like you've been here.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.200
Last week? Wasn't last week win, lose, or draw?

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:49.260
Yeah, win, lose, or draw, but we didn't tell you then is what Sean's saying.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:50.920
Oh, I see. Yeah, yeah.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.400
And, of course, I asked if you guys would rather do the episode of Take the Physical Challenge, because that is something that happens in Double Dare.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:10.420
Essentially, it's a game show where a trivia question is asked, and the kid contestants can either choose to answer the question and potentially be wrong, or, if they really don't know it, dare the other team to answer it.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:23.640
The points that are doubled, and that team can double dare back to the other kids for now double the points of the double, so quadrupling the amount of money that the trivia question is worth.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:39.780
But if the kids still don't feel like answering for all that additional money, they'll have to take the physical challenge, which is usually some fun hijinks thing of, like, catch some, you know, eggs in your burlap sack or something, or, you know, mop the floor, but with your knees.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:41.000
It's all fun stuff.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.420
Yeah, there's a lot of egg-based stuff going on here.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:46.320
A lot of food instead of, like, normal objects.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:48.840
That seems to be the running theme.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:52.600
Yeah, and essentially, it's been a running theme on Double Dare since the beginning, too.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:03:03.380
The show and its many iterations of Double Dare, Super Sloppy Double Dare, Family Double Dare, Double Dare 2000, and the Double Dare reboot in 2018, I think.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:10.180
All of these are essentially the same exact format of trivia and physical challenges.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:20.400
And I guess that leads my question to, is Double Dare a show you think fondly of, whether it's because of your childhood or just because you like the format?

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.340
Yeah, I have vague memories of Double Dare.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:26.340
I haven't watched it any time in the recent past.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:37.780
But when I did watch it, I didn't have a fully developed brain, so I'm not really sure I remembered all of the rules, or even that there were rules.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:45.500
Just that there was a boring part where they asked kids questions, and then a fun part where they threw pies at each other and stuff.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:49.840
Yeah, I mean, similarly, I do remember watching it as a kid.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:51.280
I remember enjoying it.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:55.180
It was not my favorite show or anything, but I remember when it was on, I would give it a watch.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:02.740
But it wasn't until I was older, specifically, really, when I played this game, because I haven't thought about Double Dare in a while,

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:10.360
that I kind of realized that the actual, the question part, while it's not the fun part, is a pretty genius little formula.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.700
Where it's like, yeah, you can answer it and maybe lose.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:16.740
You can dare the other team, but maybe give them more points.

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:25.860
And then if it gets dared back to you, it's like, well, I didn't know it the first time, but maybe I'll risk it for double the points, or triple the points, or accidentally give it to them.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:28.400
Like, there's a lot to think about in that one simple mechanic.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:33.220
So I think that I didn't, that was kind of lost on me as a kid.

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:42.280
I, like you, Sean, was just like, I just want to see them, you know, run through, like, a giant cream pie, or whatever, and like, look for a thing.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.280
Was that Double Dare? Yep, that was Double Dare.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:46.600
Yeah, they would have them search through things to find flags.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:48.160
I think that was Pictionary.

00:04:48.860 --> 00:05:00.160
Yeah. But no, I agree, because it doesn't, there's gamesmanship here, and it definitely did not cross my mind when I was actually watching it as a kid.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:05.860
But it also, it only works because it can't work halfway.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:14.940
If you only could just, like, dare someone, then you would just guess, because there's no, there's only ways to lose in that scenario.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:23.180
I'd rather get $0, and then mostly get $0, than get $0, and then maybe getting $20.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:28.340
If you're confident they don't know it, then they will give you, like, if they get it wrong, you get the points.

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:30.380
You get double the double the points.

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:35.180
No, no, but not even if they, not even if they double their back to you, like, if you only had.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.660
Oh, yes. Yeah, like, it still could be like, well, I know they don't know it.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:43.140
But I'm sure that'd be a rare circumstance, but, like, then if they get it wrong, you still get their points.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:48.960
Just thinking about the format of the show for a second, forget about the video game version, which has all the same rules.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:54.800
You know, from a development standpoint, it is a really interesting decision to do the whole physical challenge thing,

00:05:54.820 --> 00:06:02.220
because it creates an environment where, yeah, it's trivia, and that part, I think all three of us are saying, is pretty boring when you're a kid.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:06.140
You don't really care to learn things when you're watching these shows.

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:07.620
You just want to get on with the funny stuff.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:14.500
The fact that they made the trivia a thing where not knowing the answer isn't bad, it leads to the fun, right?

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:17.960
Like, just pass it off and hope to get the physical challenge.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:27.340
I think eventually, I don't know if the original iteration of Double Dare was like that, but by Double Dare 2000, there was a, like, everything was basically, yeah, let's just take the physical challenge.

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:28.340
Like, let's get the money.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:37.520
Like, it just got down to that point where, yeah, everybody was watching for that anyway, and I'm surprised to find out, I don't know if the game show ever, like, took this long.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:40.000
But there's 10 questions in a round?

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:45.380
Like, that's a lot of questions to also add in the physical challenges on top of that.

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:59.560
You know, it's a great decision, though, for a Nickelodeon-style kids game show to have that physical challenge component, because otherwise, this really isn't anything special.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:02.760
And I'm not even saying it's the best Nickelodeon game show.

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:14.980
I would actually give that honor to Finders Keepers, a game that didn't really get much airtime, but did get some airtime on Nick Gas, a show that, like, a channel that Sean referenced pretty recently.

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:16.840
Can't remember why you referenced it.

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:18.160
Accidentally, yes. Yeah, accidentally.

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:32.140
But Finders Keepers was, like, in 1987 or 88 or something like that, and it hit a bunch of stuff inside of a model home that kids then got to, like, ransack the entire house looking for this stuff.

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:45.940
And it was unbelievable that they had the, like, I think about the poor set designers who had to carefully design these houses filled with so many knickknacks and other things, and then just let kids destroy it in seconds.

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:47.840
And then be like, all right, set it back up again.

00:07:47.840 --> 00:08:06.220
Okay, we're going a little out in the left field here, but can you tell me what show would then give kids a shopping cart in an actual toy store and tell them they had, like, two minutes to grab whatever they wanted and they would absolutely destroy the toy store?

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:07.600
That actually wasn't a game show.

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:09.340
That was just something they did, right?

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:10.560
That was just a commercial?

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:12.000
Yeah, it was, like, a commercial.

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:15.180
They would just, no, no, no. Yeah, like, we would see it in the commercial.

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:16.920
I don't know why. It was someone won, right?

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:19.500
Yeah, it was, like, they won a sweepstake through Nickelodeon.

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:21.180
Oh, okay.

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:23.760
Good to know. That was a lot of fun.

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:26.860
I actually almost said figure it out, but figure it out didn't have that.

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:28.500
But it felt like it was. Figure it out with Summer Sanders?

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:36.080
Yes. Figure it out instead, like, you won, like, trips and bicycles and stuff and video game consoles.

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:38.180
And you weren't even really the contestant on figure it out.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:42.660
It was, like, the Knicks stars were guessing what you just hoped they lost.

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:44.160
Yeah, but you had to answer. Well, you're watching the show.

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:45.580
Yeah, you were, like, a celebrity.

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:46.820
Oh, you had to answer questions, yeah.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:48.440
But you're answering, yeah.

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:49.960
And figure it out you had a talent.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:51.340
That was the whole thing. Right.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:53.640
It wasn't, like, you know, there was something specific about you.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:57.520
So it was kind of, like, the kids' version of that show.

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:01.440
Hollywood Squares? No, no, no.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:03.880
It was, like, from way long ago, like, in the 50s.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:05.440
I can't remember what it was called.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:08.280
But they would bring on, like, famous people.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:09.500
And they would hide their faces.

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:12.060
And the celebrities would have to, like, ask them questions.

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:16.680
And it would be, like, I was the guy who was in the room when Abraham Lincoln was assassinated.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:19.220
It's, like, that's how old this TV show was.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:22.120
But that's a real example.

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:23.320
You can look up that clip on YouTube.

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:25.560
Or maybe I'll throw it in the show notes if I can remember.

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:27.940
Anyway, back to the Double Dare.

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:30.320
We've gone over the format of the show.

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:34.340
And so now, forget everything you know about how much fun it was to watch on TV.

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:37.160
Or how much you wished you were a contestant on it.

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:39.620
Now we have to talk about the video game.

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:45.800
And there's really no better way to talk about the Double Dare video game than just the setting up portion of it.

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:47.320
Where you have to pick your kid.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:51.700
It's really strange that you pick the look of your kid where you cycle through kids.

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:56.680
But then even the wording of the kid is, would you like a new character?

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:02.620
And you have to say no to basically mean, yes, like, I would like to go on with the game.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:04.200
Like, no is what you have to say to proceed.

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:06.160
It's not, do you like your character?

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:07.740
And you say yes to move on.

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:10.980
You say a negative to go forward.

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:12.480
That is weird.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:14.520
I don't think I've ever seen that before.

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:16.720
Yeah, that stood out to me.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:22.260
It didn't, I didn't think of it as all that, like, profound, though.

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:28.860
Where I think that you saying it that way makes it feel very out of the ordinary.

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:32.740
Whereas when I was just seeing it, I was just like, well, that's a weird way to do it.

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:35.260
Yeah, like, are you working in the casting department?

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:36.700
And you're like, no, this kid's no good.

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:39.320
Like, he won't test well on TV.

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:44.480
That's where, that's the actual player character is the casting agent.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:45.700
Yeah, this whole time.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:46.660
Yeah, you're the casting agent.

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:48.800
You're the cameraman. Like, you're the host.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:50.920
You're all the other things other than the kid.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:53.960
And the game can be set to one of three levels of difficulty.

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:59.120
But the only difficulty change obviously comes with the skill of the computer.

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:03.640
But also the time it takes, the time it allows you to answer questions.

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:08.960
Which, to be fair, I guess, like, yeah, they do give you a lot of time on the easy ones.

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:12.420
So it's, the questions are really like, you either know it or you don't, right?

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:15.000
Yeah, this isn't the kind of difficulty I want, though.

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:17.660
Like, I wanted it to be difficulty of the questions.

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:24.960
Because they're really, I mean, there's some, like, just more timely questions that I don't know the answer to.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:26.940
But most of these questions are pretty easy.

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:39.120
And if you've forgotten or maybe never knew the actual rules of Double Dare in which you would just try and answer all the questions correctly until you sort of figured it out yourself.

00:11:39.260 --> 00:11:42.560
This is just a boring game where you, like, win $10 at a time.

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:52.920
But even then, like, you then would say, like, okay, I don't know to an answer that you do know the answer to.

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:58.300
Just to hope that the computer rolls the die not to know it.

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:00.620
And then, yeah, and they might know it.

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:10.500
And so I think what you're getting at, Sean, very astutely in my notes, I have the same observation, is it's kind of hard to get to the fun part, the physical challenges of the game.

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:14.400
Which, as I mentioned on the TV show version, is something that happened pretty often.

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:17.340
Because, yeah, there are probably harder questions, too.

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:19.800
Yeah, and I tried for a while.

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:22.260
So I played two games of this.

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:26.300
My first game, after a while, I realized, like, wow, I'm not doing any physical challenges.

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:34.140
So I was, like, trying to constantly throw it back to the other team to try and get, like, physical challenge.

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:35.800
I would dare them and hope they'd double dare me.

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:38.660
And they either never, they would never double dare me.

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:43.300
And if they dared me and I double dared them back, they would always just answer the question.

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:47.340
Like, there was never an opportunity for a physical challenge.

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:50.180
It happened, like, twice to me in playing through both games.

00:12:50.340 --> 00:13:00.140
Well, I guess the only thing less fun than seeing the computer get a question right and take your money is watching a computer do a physical challenge that you don't have any input on.

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:02.020
So I kind of get why that was the case.

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:03.340
Yeah, but I wasn't sure.

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:07.760
I mean, I assume that the computer doesn't do a physical challenge.

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:09.360
That's what I assume.

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:12.820
But I don't have enough, I don't have a big enough sample size to confirm that.

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:24.840
Yeah, me neither. Before you even get to the physical challenges, though, or the trivia, the way that they determine who goes first is basically through a physical challenge, but something they call the toss-up challenge.

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:32.800
And the toss-up challenge, as I mentioned, determines which team gets control over the trivia round, which, since the questions are so easy, could be very crucial.

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:38.000
Unless, of course, the computer makes a mistake and then you get to dominate from there on out.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:52.380
But the toss-up challenge has this speed angle meter where it's all on one bar, but essentially speed is on the left side of the bar and angle is on the right side of the bar.

00:13:53.360 --> 00:14:00.200
And you're supposed to figure out that it works a little bit like how we think all the golf games work on NES.

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:08.880
Yes. Except for the inputs are very strange for how to start, stop, and control your speed and angle.

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:16.940
So the way it works is you hit the D-pad to start the bar moving to the speed section.

00:14:17.180 --> 00:14:20.540
Then you press the D-pad again to lock in the speed.

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:21.440
In the opposite direction.

00:14:21.560 --> 00:14:24.140
Right, in the opposite direction to push the bar the other way.

00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:26.520
The speed at which you want to throw the item.

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:31.200
And then it moves over into the angle portion, and you have to stop by pressing the A button.

00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:34.440
And now you've locked in the angle of the shot.

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:42.520
And let's forget for a second that speed and angle are, especially in your very first time playing this game, arbitrary measures.

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:45.460
Like, what does half speed mean?

00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:47.400
Like, am I throwing at half speed?

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:49.160
How far can this kid throw, right?

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:53.420
And angle. It's like, is the midpoint of angle, like, straight?

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:55.300
Or is it still going to be on an angle?

00:14:55.500 --> 00:15:01.060
And, like, you know, throwing from no angle whatsoever, like, all the way to the left side of the angle.

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:04.780
Would that be throwing to the left or throwing with no angle?

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:06.500
Like, you'll figure these things out, obviously.

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:07.900
But it's very confusing.

00:15:08.140 --> 00:15:16.300
Yeah. And there's no precision the way that NES golf works, where you're hitting, like, the A button to start, lock in your speed.

00:15:16.420 --> 00:15:17.400
Like, there's no, like, reset.

00:15:17.580 --> 00:15:24.280
It's like, once you've started it, now you're just screwed on that throw if you don't get it right from there on out.

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:25.840
And here's the thing.

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:39.540
This wouldn't be an issue if it were, if the sort of physical challenges slash, like, beginning and halftime games weren't basically all the same game.

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:48.280
Like, if this were more of a Mario Party thing where every little minigame had different mechanics and different control schemes, that'd be fine.

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:56.320
But, like, 80% of the ones that you're doing here are just variations of power and angle.

00:15:57.340 --> 00:16:01.480
You get a couple where it's a bit more freeform, but not really.

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:08.840
The fact that all of them are like this just makes that even more annoying.

00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:11.740
I do want to hear what Joe has to say.

00:16:11.740 --> 00:16:26.720
